  abstract = {Franchisees within large branded chains loudly complain of a form of\textbackslash nchannel conflict known as ``encroachment\textbraceleft ''\textbraceright{} or ``impact.\textbraceleft ''\textbraceright\textbackslash nEncroachment occurs when franchisors add new units of their brand\textbackslash nproximately to their franchisees' existing units. Franchisees claim that\textbackslash ntheir revenues have substantially decreased as a result of encroaching\textbackslash nsame-brand entry. The topic, of encroachment has not only dominated\textbackslash nfranchisee association agendas and trade journal headlines but has also\textbackslash nbecome a hot topic for politicians and policymakers. Yet, until now,\textbackslash nevidence of encroachment has been strictly anecdotal. This paper\textbackslash nprovides the first systematic evidence of encroachment. Using revenue\textbackslash ndata from the Texas lodging industry in the 1990s, I find that when\textbackslash nfranchisors approve new same-brand units in the vicinity of incumbent\textbackslash nunits, these new units cannibalize the incumbents' revenues. In contrast\textbackslash nto the result for franchisors, the addition of a new unit by\textbackslash ncompany-owned brands in the vicinity of same-brand units is associated\textbackslash nwith an increase in the incumbents' revenues. This contrast suggests\textbackslash nthat encroaching behavior is caused by incentives that result from the\textbackslash ngovernance form of franchising and is not simply an outcome that\textbackslash naccompanies all expansion. This finding informs theory on governance\textbackslash nforms and exclusive territories. Implications for practitioners and\textbackslash npolicy are also discussed.},
